  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how the degree of molecular order changes across three material states and how this affects shape switching. At the top, rod-like molecules are shown highly aligned in a crystalline state, partly aligned along a common direction in a liquid-crystalline state, and randomly oriented in an isotropic state. A blue gradient labeled “Order” decreases from left to right, emphasizing this progressive loss of alignment. In the middle state, an arrow marked *n* indicates the preferred orientation, or director, of the rods.\n\nThe lower part connects these internal arrangements to macroscopic shape changes between “State A” and “State B” under two opposite stimuli. For the more ordered liquid-crystalline case, the material changes anisotropically: the rectangle elongates in one direction while contracting in the perpendicular direction, shown by inward and outward arrows around the shape. In contrast, for the isotropic case, the object changes size more uniformly, shrinking from a larger square to a smaller square without a preferred direction. Overall, the figure conveys that molecular alignment controls whether a stimulus produces directional deformation or isotropic volume-like change.",
